          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The expression directly presents Lorck's love for Constance as a true current emotional state. His immediate insistence that it is serious reinforces its force as an avowal, irrespective of sincerity.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig, Constance  det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression avows love but does not itself undertake an obligation or relationship. Lorck's offer of himself, his life, and his soul is a separate neighbouring act and should not be transferred onto the love expression.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg giver dig mig selv, mit Liv, min Sjæl, -\\nvil du ikke ta imod det?\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Although Lorck is intensely affected, the expression is framed as a deliberate assertion whose seriousness he explicitly explains, rather than as a reflex-like or non-assertive emotional discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"det er Alvor,\\nganske anderledes end jeg selv endog vidste.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"No core function beyond the avowal represented by T is needed. In context it supports Lorck's courtship and separate offer, but those contextual purposes do not constitute an additional force of the focal expression itself.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker dig\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lorck addresses Constance during an increasingly coercive declaration of passion. After she has resisted him and asked him to stop, he repeats that he loves her, insists that it is serious, and then separately offers her himself, his life, and his soul. Constance rejects the offer and withdraws.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression itself contains no undertaking. Immediately afterward Lorck separately offers Constance himself, his life, and his soul and asks whether she will accept. The exact relational or practical terms are unspecified, and Constance explicitly rejects the offer with “Nej, uf nej,”. A still later assurance that he will not trouble her in this way again is also separate from the focal expression.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The passage appears to be from Amalie Skram's novel Constance Ring. That recognition was not used to supplement the supplied evidence.\",\"limitations\":\"The excerpt strongly represents Lorck as passionately affected, but it cannot conclusively establish his sincerity or the precise nature of the relationship he is proposing. The supplied text also contains OCR and typographical corruption, though the focal exchange remains intelligible.\"}"
        }
